<p>A shop caches its products, sessions and rendered pages in one <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term> server. Over two years the cache has outgrown the server's memory, and at the evening peak its one main thread is busy most of the time. The obvious answer is more servers, each holding part of the cache. Then, on the first day of a sale, one product's key receives a tenth of all reads, and the server that holds it is overloaded while the others are half idle. Adding servers cannot spread that one key's reads.</p>

<p>Both problems are about where keys live. Splitting a cache between servers, called <strong>sharding</strong>, raises questions a single server never asks: which server holds a key, what happens to the cache when a server is added, which commands still work when their keys are on different servers, and what clients see when a server dies. And sharding spreads keys, not requests: a key read far more often than the others, a <strong>hot key</strong>, still lands on one server.</p>

<p>This article answers those questions for Redis Cluster, Redis's built-in way of sharding. By the end you will be able to decide whether a cache needs more than one server, design keys that work in a cluster, add a shard to a running cache without emptying it, and choose the settings that decide what a node failure costs. You will be able to find hot keys with the tools in Redis 8.2 to 8.6, and relieve them, including with a cache inside the application that Redis itself keeps up to date. I ran a small cluster of Redis 8.10.3 servers on my laptop for this article; each figure says which of its numbers I measured and which come from a model.</p>

<h2 id="one-server">When one server is not enough</h2>

<p>One Redis server has three limits. The first is memory: a cache holds what fits in one machine's RAM, less what Redis needs for itself. The second is its main thread. <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works">How Redis works</ArticleRef> showed that one thread executes every command, and measured about 130,000 simple <code>GET</code>s a second on my laptop, almost all of it spent reading and writing sockets. I/O threads raise that, but every command still runs on the one thread. The third is the network: a server sending large values can fill its network link before its thread is busy.</p>

<p>Replicas, the subject of <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef>, help with only some of this. A replica holds a full copy of the data, so it adds no memory. Every write still goes to the one primary, so it adds no write capacity. It can serve reads, though those reads may be slightly out of date. What a replica buys is survival: a copy ready to take over when the primary dies.</p>

<p>Sharding addresses all three limits. Each of N primaries holds about 1/N of the keys and receives about 1/N of the requests, so memory and throughput both grow with N, as long as requests are spread evenly. The Redis Cluster specification says you can expect "the same performance as a single Redis instance multiplied by N". That condition, spread evenly, is what the second half of this article is about.</p>

<p>Sharding has costs. A cluster needs at least three primaries (the reason comes in the section on failure), more machines to watch, and client libraries that understand it. Some commands stop working, as we will see. So if the cache fits comfortably in one server's memory and its main thread has headroom at peak, one primary with a replica is simpler, and you can skip the sections on clusters. Hot keys, the tools that find them and near caches, from <a href="#hot-keys">Hot keys and hot shards</a> onwards, matter on a single server too.</p>

<h2 id="placing-keys">Placing keys on servers</h2>

<p>Suppose you have four servers. The simplest way to choose a server for a key is to hash the key to a number and take the remainder after dividing by four: <code>hash(key) mod 4</code>. Every client computes the same answer, the keys spread evenly, and nobody has to store a table of where each key lives.</p>

<p>Now add a fifth server. Every key's server becomes <code>hash(key) mod 5</code>. A key stays where it was only if its hash leaves the same remainder when divided by 4 and by 5. Counting through hash values 0 to 19, that happens for 0, 1, 2 and 3 and for none of the others: four values in twenty, or one in five. So four keys in five change server. In general, going from N servers to N + 1, a key keeps its server for one hash value in N + 1, and</p>

<p class="cluster-formula">share of keys that move = N ÷ (N + 1)<br />4 → 5 servers: 4 ÷ 5 = 80%</p>

<p>In a database that would mean copying 80% of the data. In a cache nobody copies anything: the key's new server has never seen it, so the next read is a <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term> and goes to the database. Adding a server to relieve the cache empties four-fifths of it at once, and the database takes the load the cache was carrying. Figure 1 shows this. Each cell is a cached key, labelled with its server.</p>

<PlacementFigure number={1} />

<p>With "hash mod N" chosen, most cells change. Move the slider to 8 servers and it gets worse: 89% of keys move when the ninth arrives. The more servers you have, the more of the cache one more server empties.</p>

<p>The classic fix is <strong>consistent hashing</strong>, from a 1997 paper by Karger and others. Servers and keys are both hashed onto a circle, and each key belongs to the next server clockwise. Each server is placed at many points on the circle, 160 in this figure, so that it owns many short arcs rather than one long one. A new server takes over only the arcs just before its own points, and every other key stays put. Choose "a hash ring": going from four servers to five, 18% of the keys change server. On average a new server takes 1/(N + 1) of the circle, a fifth here. The keys that move still miss, because their new server is empty, but the rest of the cache is untouched.</p>

<p>Redis Cluster does something different again. It hashes every key to one of 16,384 <strong>hash slots</strong>, and each server owns some of the slots. The mapping from key to slot never changes. Adding a server means moving some slots to it, and Redis moves each slot together with the keys in it. Choose "hash slots": a fifth of the keys change server, about as many as with the ring, but they are drawn solid because their data went with them. None of them misses. The cost moves from the database to the network: the moved keys have to be copied, which a later section measures.</p>

<h2 id="hash-slots">Hash slots</h2>

<p>Redis computes a key's slot from CRC16, a 16-bit checksum of the key's name, by taking the remainder after dividing by 16,384:</p>

<p class="cluster-formula">slot = CRC16(key) mod 16384</p>

<p>Each primary owns a range of slots, or several ranges. When <code>redis-cli --cluster create</code> built my three-primary cluster, it gave the first slots 0 to 5460, the second 5461 to 10922 and the third 10923 to 16383. Every node knows the whole map, and so does every cluster-aware client, which can then send each command straight to the node that owns its key.</p>

<p>Why 16,384? Every message between nodes carries a bitmap of the sender's slots, and 16,384 bits is 2 kilobytes. Salvatore Sanfilippo, who designed Cluster, chose it as enough for clusters of up to about 1,000 primaries, which is the size Cluster is designed for.</p>

<p>Figure 2 computes slots in your browser with the same CRC16. Type keys separated by spaces, or start from an example. Each key's slot appears as a mark on the slot bar and as a row in the table, with the part of the key that was hashed highlighted.</p>

<KeySlotFigure number={2} />

<p>Notice first that similar names land far apart. <code>product:42</code> is in slot 12456 on node C and <code>product:43</code> in slot 8329 on node B. That is what a good hash is for: it spreads keys evenly, whatever their names. The CRC16 here matched <code>CLUSTER KEYSLOT</code> on my cluster for every key I tried.</p>

<p>Sometimes you want keys together, because one command or transaction must touch them all. A <strong>hash tag</strong> does this: if a key contains <code>{'{'}</code> and, somewhere after it, <code>{'}'}</code>, with at least one character between them, only the text between the first <code>{'{'}</code> and the first <code>{'}'}</code> after it is hashed. Choose "One user, hash tag": <code>{'user:{123}:profile'}</code> and <code>{'user:{123}:cart'}</code> hash only <code>123</code>, so both land in slot 5970. Only the first tag counts, and empty braces are no tag; the "Odd braces" example shows these edge cases.</p>

<p>Now choose "Tag on the wrong part". <code>{'{user}:123:profile'}</code> and <code>{'{user}:456:profile'}</code> also share a slot, 5474, because the tag is the word <code>user</code>. So does every other key written this way: every user's profile in one slot on one node. A hash tag gathers keys deliberately, and the same mechanism will gather a whole class of keys by accident. Put the tag around the part that identifies the group, usually an id: <code>{'user:{123}:profile'}</code>, not <code>{'{user}:123:profile'}</code>.</p>

<h2 id="what-changes">What a cluster cannot do</h2>

<p>A command that touches several keys runs on one node, so all its keys must be on that node. Redis Cluster asks for more than that: they must all be in the same slot. Otherwise the command fails with a <Term kind="error" mark>CROSSSLOT</Term> error, even when both slots happen to be on the same node, because slots can move independently and a command that worked today would fail after the next reshard. On my cluster:</p>

<pre><code>{`> MGET product:42 product:43
CROSSSLOT Keys in request don't hash to the same slot
> MSET cart:{u9}:items 3 cart:{u9}:total 4200
OK
> SELECT 1
ERR SELECT is not allowed in cluster mode`}</code></pre>

<p>The rule reaches further than <code>MGET</code>:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Batched reads.</strong> <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining">Redis clients</ArticleRef> cut a product page's 24 round trips to one with <code>MGET</code>. In a cluster, redis-py 8.1's <code>mget</code> refused keys in different slots; its <code>mget_nonatomic</code> and its cluster pipeline split the request into one batch per node. That is the right default for cache reads: one round trip per node, not per key, and nothing about a batch of independent reads needs to be atomic.</li>
  <li><strong>Transactions.</strong> A <code>MULTI</code> block whose keys are in different slots failed on my cluster, with <code>MOVED</code> as a command was queued or <code>CROSSSLOT</code> at <code>EXEC</code>, and none of it ran.</li>
  <li><strong>Scripts.</strong> A Lua script must declare its keys in <code>KEYS</code>, as <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="scripts">Redis clients</ArticleRef> explained, and they too must share a slot; my two-key <code>EVAL</code> failed with <code>CROSSSLOT</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>One database.</strong> A cluster has only database 0. If you separate the cache from other data with <code>SELECT 1</code>, use a key prefix instead.</li>
  <li><strong>Walking the keys.</strong> <code>SCAN</code> on one node returns only that node's keys. To walk a whole cluster, scan every primary.</li>
</ul>

<p>Look at the earlier articles' patterns through this rule. A lock key and the value it protects, a value and its version counter, a tag set and the pages it lists, a generation counter and the keys that include it: wherever a script or transaction reads or writes two keys together, those keys need one hash tag. <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation</ArticleRef>'s tag-set script, for example, needs every page key and the tag's set in one slot. Give each group its own tag, such as the product id, so that groups still spread across the cluster. Keys you only read together in a batch need no tag: the client splits the batch per node.</p>

<h2 id="redirections">Finding the right node: MOVED and ASK</h2>

<p>A cluster client starts by asking any node for the slot map, with <code>CLUSTER SHARDS</code> (Redis 7.0 and later) or the older <code>CLUSTER SLOTS</code>, which many clients, redis-py 8.1 among them, still use. It then sends each command to the node its map names. But slots move, and nodes fail, so a map can be out of date. A node that receives a command for a slot it does not own does not forward it. It replies with a redirection, and the client sends the command again to the right node.</p>

<p>There are two redirections, and they mean different things. Figure 3 steps through both, using replies I received from my cluster while moving slot 12456, which holds <code>product:42</code>, from node C to node A.</p>

<RedirectFigure number={3} />

<p>Step through "has moved" first. <code>MOVED</code> says the slot belongs to another node now, for good. The client should update its map, and a good client reloads the whole map, because slots usually move in batches. After that, requests for the slot go straight to the new owner. Each <code>MOVED</code> costs one extra round trip, and a client that has just reloaded its map rarely meets another soon after.</p>

<p>Now step through "is moving". This happens during a key-by-key migration, the way slots moved before Redis 8.4. While a slot is migrating, the old node still owns it and has some of its keys; the new node has the others. A key the old node still has, it serves. For a key it does not have, whether already sent across or never there, it replies <code>ASK</code>: try the other node, for this one command. So during a migration every cache miss in the slot costs an extra round trip too. The client sends <code>ASKING</code> and then the command to the new node, and leaves its map alone, because the slot has not finished moving. A command whose keys are split between the two nodes cannot be answered by either and gets <code>TRYAGAIN</code>, so the client waits and retries.</p>

<p>A cluster-aware client library does all of this for you. What you see is latency: an extra round trip for each redirection, and retries for <code>TRYAGAIN</code>. A steady trickle of <code>MOVED</code> replies while slots are moving is normal. A steady stream of them when nothing is moving means a client that is not updating its map.</p>

<p>Two practical consequences. A cluster client keeps a connection to each primary it talks to, which in practice is all of them, and to replicas if it reads from them, so 200 application processes and 12 primaries make at least 2,400 connections. And not every application talks to the cluster directly. A proxy such as Envoy's Redis proxy can sit in between: the application sees one server, and the proxy follows redirections and splits multi-key reads by key, at the cost of an extra network hop on every request. Some managed services work this way behind a single endpoint.</p>

<h2 id="resharding">Resharding and slot migration</h2>

<p>Moving slots to a new primary is called <strong>resharding</strong>. Until Redis 8.4 it worked key by key: mark the slot as migrating on the old node and importing on the new one, then copy its keys across in small batches with <code>MIGRATE</code>, which sends each key, waits for the new node to confirm it, then deletes its own copy, and finally tell every node that the slot has a new owner. Between the first key and the last, clients meet <code>ASK</code> and <code>TRYAGAIN</code>, and a slot of millions of keys takes a long time.</p>

<p>Redis 8.4 added <strong>atomic slot migration</strong>, which moves a range of slots as one unit: the old node copies the slots whole, keeps the copy up to date, and hands over ownership only when the new node has everything. Figure 4 steps through it for one slot.</p>

<MigrationFigure number={4} />

<p>Notice what the client never meets: an <code>ASK</code> or a <code>TRYAGAIN</code>. Until the handoff, the old node owns the slot and holds every key in it, so it answers everything; after the handoff, the new node holds every key, and the client meets one <code>MOVED</code> and updates its map. The 8.10.3 <code>redis-cli --cluster reshard</code> uses atomic migration without being asked: its source checks that every node runs 8.4.0 or later and then sends <code>CLUSTER MIGRATION IMPORT</code>. Valkey 9.0 also moves whole slots atomically, with its own commands.</p>

<p>I measured both on my laptop. I built a cluster of three primaries holding 200,000 keys of 500 bytes, about 66,600 keys on each, added an empty fourth primary, and moved a quarter of all slots to it while four clients read random keys at 1,000 reads a second each. I ran each method once, on a fresh cluster with the same keys. The atomic move took 3.1 seconds. The key-by-key move took 220 seconds, and the clients ran for only its first 50. So the second row compares the two over the same stretch, the first 3.1 seconds of each move, and the last three give the counts for each whole load run, which covered different parts of the two moves.</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Measured cost of moving a quarter of the slots to a new primary">
