<p>Every read was a <Term kind="hit" mark>hit</Term> in both runs, because the keys moved with their slots, as Figure 1 promised, and no client saw an error. The key-by-key time is only indicative: my script was Python, on the same laptop as the clients. Redis's own published test, a third of the slots of a 5 GB cluster (about 1.7 GB) moved to a fourth shard, reports 6.4 seconds for atomic migration against 192 to 219 seconds key by key. That is the vendor's benchmark, but it points the same way. It also shows that the time grows with the data moved, because the snapshot is streamed: my move was about 25 MB.</p>

<p>So on Redis 8.4 or later, add a shard with <code>redis-cli --cluster reshard</code>, or with <code>rebalance --cluster-use-empty-masters</code> (without that flag, <code>rebalance</code> gives an empty primary nothing), and expect a brief burst of <code>MOVED</code>. On older versions, reshard slowly, outside the peak, and expect <code>ASK</code> and <code>TRYAGAIN</code>. In either case the new node needs room for the slots it receives, and the old node forks to take its snapshot, which costs memory as <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="rdb">snapshots</ArticleRef> do.</p>

<h2 id="failover">Cluster failover</h2>

<p>Each primary in a cluster can have replicas, and the cluster fails over by itself: there is no Sentinel. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="sentinel">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> describes failover with Sentinel; Cluster follows the same outline with three differences that matter.</p>

<p>First, the nodes watch each other. Each node pings a few others every second over a separate connection, the <strong>cluster bus</strong>, on the data port plus 10,000, and pings any it has not heard from for half the timeout. A node that has not answered for <code>cluster-node-timeout</code>, 15 seconds by default, is flagged <code>PFAIL</code> (possibly failing) by the node that noticed. Nodes pass these flags to each other in their regular messages, and once one node has collected reports from a majority of the primaries, it marks the silent node <code>FAIL</code> and tells the rest. Both flags appear in <code>CLUSTER NODES</code> output and in the logs. Only <code>FAIL</code> starts an election among the failed primary's replicas. That majority is why a cluster needs at least three primaries: with two, losing one leaves no majority.</p>

<p>Second, the replicas elect themselves. Each replica of the failed primary waits before asking for votes: half a second, plus a random amount up to another half second, plus a second for each replica that has more of the primary's data than it does. So the most up-to-date replica usually asks first, and wins when a majority of primaries vote for it. In my run, the replica logged that its election was "delayed for 883 milliseconds (rank #0)" and won it 1.1 seconds after the failure was agreed.</p>

<p>Third, by default each node stops serving when any slot is unassigned or owned by a node marked <code>FAIL</code>. Figure 5 shows what that means. I killed one primary of a three-primary cluster and recorded how many reads succeeded, a second, for keys on each shard. Choose what the dead primary had.</p>

<FailoverFigure number={5} />

<p>With a replica, the failed shard's keys were unavailable for 21 seconds: 15 seconds before nodes began flagging the primary <code>PFAIL</code>, a few more while those reports spread to a majority and made it <code>FAIL</code> (20 seconds after the kill in this run), and about one more for the election. Look at the other line just before the replica is promoted. The healthy shards also served nothing for about a second, because between the failure being agreed and the promotion, slots 0 to 5460 belonged to a node marked as failed, and every node refused every request.</p>

<p>Now choose "had no replica". Once the failure was agreed, after 19.9 seconds, the whole cluster stopped. Reads of keys on the two healthy shards failed with <code>CLUSTERDOWN The cluster is down</code> until the end of the run, and would have until someone fixed it. For a database that refusal is a safety feature: an application should not see a database with a third of its data missing. For a cache it is usually the wrong trade, because a missing third of the cache is just a third of reads going to the database.</p>

<p>The setting <code>cluster-require-full-coverage no</code> changes it. Choose the last option: the dead shard's keys stayed unavailable, and the other two shards served every read throughout. A related setting, <code>cluster-allow-reads-when-down yes</code>, lets nodes serve reads but not writes while the cluster is down; in my run, reads on a healthy shard worked and a <code>SET</code> failed with <code>CLUSTERDOWN The cluster is down and only accepts read commands</code>. For a cache, set <code>cluster-require-full-coverage no</code> in every node's configuration file, replicas included, because each node decides for itself whether the cluster is up; and make your application treat a refused or failed cache read as a miss.</p>

<p>Detection took 17 to 20 seconds in four runs with the default timeout. A lower <code>cluster-node-timeout</code> fails over sooner, but it must stay longer than the longest pause a healthy node can have, or the pause itself causes a failover: a fork on a large dataset (for a snapshot, a full resync or an atomic slot migration), a slow command or script, a large <code>DEL</code>, a virtual machine stalling. A lower timeout also shortens how long a replica may have been disconnected and still be allowed to take over, which is the timeout multiplied by <code>cluster-replica-validity-factor</code>, 10 by default. So measure those pauses on your own nodes and set the timeout above them. The rest of <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="failover-loss">what a failover loses</ArticleRef> applies unchanged: replication is asynchronous, so writes and deletes the old primary acknowledged can be missing from the replica that takes over.</p>

<p>Replicas also decide how often the cluster stops. With one replica per primary, the cluster specification works out that losing two random nodes leaves some slots with no node to serve them with probability 1/(2N − 1) for N primaries: one in five for three primaries, about one in eleven for six. Redis can move a spare replica to a primary that has lost all of its own (<strong>replica migration</strong>), so one extra replica protects the whole cluster better than it protects one shard.</p>

<p>Two more differences from Sentinel matter in operation. To restart a primary on purpose, send <code>CLUSTER FAILOVER</code> to one of its replicas: the replica waits until it has caught up, then swaps roles with the primary, losing no acknowledged writes. And a primary cut off from the majority of primaries stops accepting writes once the node timeout has passed, and reads too unless <code>cluster-allow-reads-when-down</code> is on, whatever the full-coverage setting, because it cannot tell whether the majority has already replaced it.</p>

<h2 id="hot-keys">Hot keys and hot shards</h2>

<p>Everything so far has assumed that requests spread across shards as evenly as keys do. They do not. In most caches a few keys are read far more often than the rest: the products on the home page, the configuration every request reads, the item in the sale. The <ArticleRef slug="" hash="skew">introduction</ArticleRef> modelled this with a <strong>Zipf distribution</strong>: the key ranked k receives a share of requests proportional to 1/k<sup>s</sup>, where s, the skew, has to be measured. Breslau and others found s between 0.64 and 0.83 in six web proxy traces; your own access logs will tell you how skewed your cache is. I use s = 1 below because its arithmetic is easy, not because it is typical, and Figure 6 lets you try 0.8 and 1.2.</p>

<p>With s = 1, the most popular of N keys receives 1/H<sub>N</sub> of all requests, where H<sub>N</sub> = 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + … + 1/N. For 100,000 keys, H<sub>N</sub> is about 12.09, so</p>

<p class="cluster-formula">share of the hottest key = 1 ÷ 12.09 ≈ 8.3%<br />at 600,000 reads a second: 0.083 × 600,000 ≈ 49,600 reads a second, on one key</p>

<p>That key lives in one slot on one node. However many shards you add, it brings its 49,600 reads a second with it. Figure 6 is a model of this: 600,000 reads a second over 100,000 product keys, each key placed by its real hash slot, and every node able to serve an assumed 100,000 reads a second. Each column is one shard's load, and the solid block at the foot of one column is the hottest key's reads.</p>

<HotShardFigure number={6} />

<p>Start at 3 shards. The average is 200,000 reads a second per shard, above any node's capacity, so the cluster is too small anyway, but notice that one column stands above the others. Move to 6, then 12 shards. The average halves each time. The tallest column falls too, from 250,000 to about 138,000 and then 100,000, but more slowly, and the solid block at its foot does not shrink at all. At 12 shards the average is 50,000 and the busiest node takes about 100,000, just over its limit, while eleven others have room to spare. Keep going: at 48 shards the average is 12,500 and the busiest still takes about 75,000, the hottest key's 49,600 plus the other popular keys that share its node. Adding shards takes other keys away from the hot key's node, but never the hot key.</p>

<p>The readout "Usable share of total capacity" is the average divided by the busiest: how full the cluster is when its busiest node is full. It falls from 80% at 3 shards to 17% at 48. A cluster stops working well when its busiest node is full, not when its average node is, so the most it can take is</p>

<p class="cluster-formula">most reads a second = capacity of one node ÷ share of reads on the busiest node<br />48 shards: 100,000 ÷ 0.124 ≈ 800,000, from 48 nodes that could serve 4.8 million between them</p>

<p>And because the busiest node always carries at least the hottest key, no number of shards takes this workload past 100,000 ÷ 0.083, about 1.2 million reads a second. Now choose s = 1.2. The hottest key alone receives about 118,000 reads a second, more than one node can serve, and no number of shards is enough: the node holding it is overloaded at 3 shards and still overloaded at 48. With s = 0.8 the hottest key receives about 13,000, and adding shards keeps helping for longer.</p>

<p>The model is simple: reads only, every node identical, an overloaded node shown as stopping at a line rather than slowing down. But the conclusion does not depend on the details: a hot key needs a remedy of its own, and first you need to find it.</p>

<h2 id="finding-hot-keys">Finding hot keys</h2>

<p>Start with the nodes, not the keys. A hot key shows up as one primary busier than the others: compare <code>instantaneous_ops_per_sec</code> and network bytes in <code>INFO</code>, and the call counts in <code>INFO commandstats</code>, across primaries. To have something real to look at, I ran three clients reading 10,000 keys at 3,000 reads a second for three minutes, with keys chosen by a Zipf distribution with s = 1. The busiest node, port 7195, served 236,334 of the 540,042 reads, 1.31 times its fair third. The hottest key, <code>product:0</code>, received 10.2% of all reads on its own, and node 7195 also held the second, fifth and sixth hottest.</p>

<p>Which keys? Redis offers three tools, and they measure different things.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong><code>redis-cli --hotkeys</code></strong> walks every key with <code>SCAN</code> and reads each key's LFU counter, the frequency estimate described in <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="approximated-lfu">Expiration and eviction</ArticleRef>. It works only when <code>maxmemory-policy</code> is an LFU policy; otherwise it exits with "An LFU maxmemory policy is not selected". It runs against one node at a time. If you switch to LFU during an incident, give the counters some traffic before trusting them; on 8.6 or later, <code>HOTKEYS</code> needs no policy change.</li>
  <li><strong><code>HOTKEYS</code></strong>, added in Redis 8.6, records the keys that use the most processor time and network bytes during a session you start and stop, keeping an approximate top list. <code>HOTKEYS START METRICS 2 CPU NET COUNT 10</code> starts one; <code>HOTKEYS GET</code> reads it. <code>SAMPLE</code> looks at only one key in some ratio, to lower the cost, and <code>SLOTS</code> limits it to chosen slots. It reports on one node, and its reference notes that the redis-py and go-redis cluster clients do not support it, so connect to each primary directly.</li>
  <li><strong><code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS</code></strong>, added in Redis 8.2, reports per slot: the number of keys, and, when <code>cluster-slot-stats-enabled</code> is on, the processor time and network bytes spent on the slot (and its memory, from 8.4). <code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS ORDERBY cpu-usec LIMIT 10</code> lists the busiest slots. It names the slot, not the key: on 8.6 or later, <code>HOTKEYS START … SLOTS 1 &lt;slot&gt;</code> on that node then names the key. It tells you which slots to move. Like the others it reports on one node, so run it on every primary. Memory tracking can only be turned on in the configuration file, but on 8.10.3 <code>CONFIG SET cluster-slot-stats-enabled "cpu net"</code> worked on a running node, so you can turn on the other two during an incident. Redis's documentation lists the command as unsupported on Redis Software and Redis Cloud.</li>
</ul>

<p>Figure 7 compares what they reported for the busiest node's keys with the true request counts, which my clients recorded.</p>

<ToolsFigure number={7} />

<p>Choose "LFU counter". The true counts run from 55,126 for <code>product:0</code> to 3,302 for <code>product:16</code>, about 17 to 1. The LFU counter reports 107 and 29, under 4 to 1, and puts <code>product:5</code> above <code>product:4</code>. The counter is logarithmic by design: it grows ever more slowly so that eight bits can rank millions of reads. That makes <code>--hotkeys</code> good for spotting the top few keys and useless for saying how much load each carries.</p>

<p>Choose "HOTKEYS bytes": the same order as the true counts, in proportion, because every read of a 300-byte value sends the same number of bytes. Choose "HOTKEYS CPU": nearly the same order, but with <code>product:4976</code>, read 17 times in three minutes, in fifth place, charged about 430 µs a call when the other keys' <code>GET</code>s cost under 1 µs. I did not find the cause; it looks like time from something else charged to that key. So read the CPU ranking with care, and prefer the bytes ranking when values are the same size. <code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS</code> reported the same processor times per slot as <code>HOTKEYS</code> per key.</p>

<p>One tool to avoid on a busy node is <code>MONITOR</code>, which streams every command to your terminal. Its documentation warns that one <code>MONITOR</code> client can reduce a server's throughput by more than half; on an already overloaded node that makes the overload worse. Sampling on the client side, in the application, costs Redis nothing and is often the quickest way to name the key.</p>

<p>A hot shard can also come from a big key rather than a busy one: one large value or collection skews a node's memory and network traffic in the same way, and sharding cannot split it either. <code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS</code> shows each slot's key count and, with memory tracking, its memory, and <code>redis-cli --bigkeys</code> and <code>--memkeys</code> find the keys themselves.</p>

<h2 id="relieving">Relieving a hot key</h2>

<p>Once you know the key, there are four common remedies, and they differ in what they cost. Figure 8 applies each to the model of Figure 6 at 12 shards, where the busiest node is just over its limit.</p>

<RemediesFigure number={8} />

<p>Choose "copies of hot keys". The 10 hottest keys are stored four times each, as <code>product:0:0</code> to <code>product:0:3</code> and so on, in different slots, and each read picks a copy at random. Choose the suffixes so that the copies' slots are on different nodes, and check with <code>CLUSTER KEYSLOT</code>: four copies in random slots on twelve shards share a node about 40% of the time, and then relieve less. The busiest node falls from about 100,000 to about 56,000 reads a second, and 89% of capacity becomes usable. Facebook's memcache paper describes a related idea: when a pool's whole data set fits in one or two servers and requests fetch many keys at once, it replicates the keys across the pool's servers rather than splitting them, to spread the request rate. The cost is on the write side: every update must write or delete every copy, and for a moment some copies are newer than others. Use it for keys that are read constantly and changed rarely.</p>

<p>Choose "read from replicas". Each shard gets a replica, and reads are split between them. In a cluster, a client reads from a replica by sending <code>READONLY</code> on its connection; the replica then serves reads for its own primary's slots and still answers <code>MOVED</code> for others. The busiest node halves, to about 50,000, but so does every other, and the cluster now has 24 nodes: the usable share stays at 50%. Replicas help when a whole shard is busy; for a single key, each replica adds at most one node's worth of reads, and those reads can be <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="replication">slightly out of date</ArticleRef>.</p>

<p>Choose "near cache". Each of 40 application processes keeps the 1,000 hottest keys in its own memory for one second, so Redis sees at most 40 reads a second for each of them, one per process per second. The reads reaching Redis fall from 600,000 to about 270,000 a second, and the busiest node takes about 23,000, almost exactly the average. This is the strongest remedy by far, because it removes the hot reads instead of spreading them, and it needs no change to Redis. Its cost is staleness: each process may serve a value up to a second old, and different processes may disagree for that second. The next sections show how Redis can shorten that.</p>

<p>Two other remedies need no figure. If a hot key shares its node with other busy slots, move those slots away with a reshard, so the hot key has a whole node to itself; <code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS</code> shows which slots are busy. And if the hot key is written rather than read, such as a counter of views, split it into several keys, say <code>views:42:0</code> to <code>views:42:7</code>, increment a random one, and add them up when you read. Leave the parts without a hash tag, so that they land in different slots and spread across nodes; with the slot ranges of my three-primary cluster, these eight land in eight different slots across all three nodes. Reading the total then takes one request per node, which <code>mget_nonatomic</code> or a cluster pipeline sends for you. A hash tag such as <code>{'views:{42}:0'}</code> would put every part back in one slot on one node, whose one thread would do the same work as before, and relieve nothing.</p>

<p>The usual order is: near cache first for keys that are read constantly and can be a second or so out of date, copies for keys that must be fresher or are read by many services, replicas when a whole shard is hot rather than one key, and moving slots when the hot key's node is crowded.</p>

<h2 id="near-cache">Client-side caching with CLIENT TRACKING</h2>

<p>A <strong>near cache</strong>, or client-side cache, is a small cache inside each application process, in front of Redis. A hit there costs no network round trip at all, only a lookup in the process's own memory. The <ArticleRef slug="">introduction</ArticleRef> pointed out the problem: each process has its own copy, and when the value changes, nothing tells the copies. A short time to live bounds how stale a copy can be, which is what the one-second near cache in Figure 8 relies on. But a short time to live also means frequent reads from Redis, and some values cannot be a second out of date.</p>

<p>Redis 6.0 added a way for Redis to tell the near caches: <strong>server-assisted client-side caching</strong>, turned on per connection with <code>CLIENT TRACKING ON</code>. Redis then remembers which keys that connection has read, and when any client changes one of them, it sends the connection an <Term kind="write" mark>invalidation</Term> message naming the key. Figure 9 steps through the messages, which I recorded from Redis 8.10.3.</p>

<TrackingFigure number={9} />

<p>Step through the default mode and watch Redis's state under its heading. When App A reads <code>product:42</code>, Redis records the read. When App B changes the key, Redis pushes an invalidation to A and forgets that A read it. B's second write sends nothing, because A has not read the key again. So each read earns at most one invalidation, and a key A never read is never announced. In my tests a <code>DEL</code>, an <code>EXPIRE</code> that set a new time to live, and the key expiring all counted as changes, and <code>FLUSHALL</code> sent an invalidation with no key, meaning "drop everything".</p>

<p>On my laptop the invalidation arrived a median of 268 µs after the writer sent its <code>SET</code>, and 639 µs at the 99th percentile, over 200 writes. That is the window in which A can serve the old value: a fraction of a millisecond on one machine, a little more across a network, against a second for a one-second time to live.</p>

<p>Redis's memory is the price. In the default mode Redis keeps a table of every key that tracking connections have read, with the connections that read each one, up to <code>tracking-table-max-keys</code> keys, a million by default. When the table is full, Redis makes room by invalidating keys nobody has changed. I set the limit to 3 and read six keys: invalidations arrived for keys nobody had written, and the table stayed at three. That is safe, because the client just reads those keys again, but it means an invalidation only says "this key may have changed".</p>

<p>Now choose broadcast mode, turned on with <code>CLIENT TRACKING ON BCAST PREFIX product:</code>. Redis keeps no record of reads at all, only the prefixes clients have registered. Every change to a key that starts with <code>product:</code> sends an invalidation to every client that registered the prefix, whether or not it ever read the key, and keeps sending them. That costs Redis no memory per key but more messages, so it suits a small set of keys that many processes cache, such as configuration or the products on the home page.</p>

<p>Three options adjust this. <code>OPTIN</code> tracks only the one command after <code>CLIENT CACHING YES</code> (or the <code>MULTI</code> block or script that follows it), so a client must send <code>CLIENT CACHING YES</code> before each read it means to cache: after one <code>YES</code>, caching several reads means caching keys Redis is not tracking, and they stay stale. <code>NOLOOP</code> stops a client being told about its own writes. And RESP2 clients cannot receive pushed messages on a data connection, so they use a second, redirected connection (<code>CLIENT TRACKING ON REDIRECT</code>); RESP3 clients receive invalidations on the same connection, which, as the next section shows, is the safer arrangement.</p>

<p>You rarely send these commands yourself. Client libraries with a built-in near cache that uses tracking include redis-py from 5.1.0, Jedis from 5.2.0, node-redis from 5.1.0 and go-redis from 9.22.0, according to Redis's documentation, which also says these caches need a RESP3 connection and Redis 7.4 or later. redis-py 8.1 enforces this: it refuses to turn on its cache unless the server reports itself as Redis 7.4 or later. Valkey 8.0 and later reports itself as <code>valkey</code> (or, in its compatibility mode, as Redis 7.2.4), so redis-py's built-in cache will not run against it. On older Redis and on Valkey the tracking commands above still work, but you build the near cache yourself or use another library.</p>

<p>In a cluster, tracking is per connection, so the library tracks on its connection to each node, and invalidations come from the node that owns the key. One interaction with resharding, the last step of Figure 4: when any client is tracking, atomic slot migration deletes the old node's copies on its main thread rather than in the background. Tracking has no message for "this whole slot has gone", so Redis deletes the keys one at a time and sends an invalidation for each, which takes some of the main thread's time while it runs.</p>

<h2 id="near-cache-limits">What can still go wrong with a near cache</h2>

<p>Tracking makes a near cache much fresher, but not exact. Redis's client-side caching reference describes a race that appears when invalidations arrive on a different connection from the data. Figure 10 shows it. Choose whether the near cache stores every reply or uses a placeholder.</p>

<RaceFigure number={10} />

<p>Without a placeholder, the invalidation for B's write overtakes the reply to A's read, which Redis sent before the write. The invalidation arrives while A has nothing cached, so it removes nothing; then the old value arrives and A stores it. Redis has already sent its one invalidation for that read, so A serves a <Term kind="stale" mark>stale</Term> value until its own copy expires. With a placeholder, A records "fetching" before it sends the read, the invalidation removes the record, and when the reply arrives without a record waiting, A does not store it. With RESP3 and a single connection the race cannot happen, because one connection delivers messages in order.</p>

<p>The same reference lists the other ways a near cache goes wrong, and they decide how to configure one:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A lost connection loses invalidations.</strong> Redis sends each invalidation once, on one connection. If the connection drops, the messages sent while it was down are gone, so the near cache must be emptied whenever the connection is lost or reconnected. A connection can also die silently, showing no error until something is sent on it, so ping the connection that carries invalidations every few seconds, as Redis's reference advises, and empty the near cache if the replies stop. After a failover, the client connects to a new primary, which has no record of its reads, so it must empty the near cache then too.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep a time to live anyway.</strong> Give every entry in the near cache a maximum time to live, even keys that have none in Redis. If an invalidation is lost by any route, that bounds how long the stale value lasts.</li>
  <li><strong>Bound its memory.</strong> A near cache lives in the application's memory, multiplied by the number of processes. Limit it by entries or bytes, and evict the least used entries when it is full.</li>
  <li><strong>Cache the right keys.</strong> The best candidates are read often and changed occasionally. A key that changes constantly, such as a counter, sends an invalidation for nearly every read and gains nothing.</li>
</ul>

<p>With those in place, a near cache with tracking is usually the first answer to a read-heavy hot key: it takes the load off the node entirely, and after most writes its values are stale only until the invalidation arrives, under a millisecond in my tests, instead of for a whole time to live.</p>

<h2 id="decisions">What you can now decide</h2>

<p>The shop from the opening needed more memory and more throughput than one server has. A cluster of hash slots gives both, and on Redis 8.4 and later a shard can be added to the running cache without a single miss and in seconds rather than minutes: about three seconds for the 25 MB I moved and in seconds for gigabytes in Redis's own test. The sale's hot product needs something else: a near cache in the application, kept fresh by tracking, takes its reads off the cluster altogether.</p>

<p>More generally, you can now:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Decide whether a cache needs sharding at all, from its memory, the main thread's load at peak and its network traffic, remembering that replicas add neither memory nor write capacity.</li>
  <li>Explain why <code>hash mod N</code> empties most of a cache when a server is added, and why Redis Cluster's hash slots, which move with their data, do not.</li>
  <li>Design keys for a cluster: a hash tag around an id for keys that a script, transaction or multi-key command must touch together, and no tag for keys that are only read in batches.</li>
  <li>Read <code>MOVED</code>, <code>ASK</code>, <code>TRYAGAIN</code> and <code>CROSSSLOT</code>, and know which are normal and which mean a bug.</li>
  <li>Reshard with atomic slot migration on Redis 8.4 or later, and know what clients see during the older key-by-key method.</li>
  <li>Configure a cache cluster to survive a node failure: replicas for primaries, <code>cluster-require-full-coverage no</code> on every node, a <code>cluster-node-timeout</code> above your nodes' longest pauses, and an application that treats a failed cache read as a miss.</li>
  <li>Predict from a workload's skew how far adding shards will help, and find hot keys and slots with <code>INFO</code>, <code>HOTKEYS</code>, <code>CLUSTER SLOT-STATS</code> and <code>--hotkeys</code>, knowing what each measures.</li>
  <li>Relieve a hot key with a near cache, copies, replica reads, moving slots or, for a write-hot key, untagged parts, and run a near cache with tracking safely: one RESP3 connection or placeholders, emptied on disconnect, with a maximum time to live.</li>
</ul>

<p>A cluster is more to run than one server: more nodes to watch, more settings to choose, more ways to fail. What to measure, how to size the cache and how to survive its loss are the subject of <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>.</p>

